# `!b&&c||!d`: the `)` that closes `not(b` goes before the ` and` that replaces `&&`.
mode: syntax
---- query
from t | where !b&&c||!d
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..25
  PIPELINE@0..24
    IDENT@0..4 "from"
    WHITESPACE@4..5 " "
    NAME_REF@5..6
      IDENT@5..6 "t"
    WHITESPACE@6..7 " "
    PIPE@7..8 "|"
    WHITESPACE@8..9 " "
    WHERE_OP@9..24
      IDENT@9..14 "where"
      WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
      BIN_EXPR@15..24
        BIN_EXPR@15..20
          PREFIX_EXPR@15..17
            BANG@15..16 "!"
            NAME_REF@16..17
              IDENT@16..17 "b"
          AMP2@17..19 "&&"
          NAME_REF@19..20
            IDENT@19..20 "c"
        PIPE2@20..22 "||"
        PREFIX_EXPR@22..24
          BANG@22..23 "!"
          NAME_REF@23..24
            IDENT@23..24 "d"
  WHITESPACE@24..25 "\n"

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:16
  |
1 | from t | where !b&&c||!d
  |                ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(b)`

error[DS0111]: `&&` is not an operator
  --> query:1:18
  |
1 | from t | where !b&&c||!d
  |                  ^^ this is C's "and"
  |
  = help: use `and` to require both conditions

error[DS0111]: `||` is not an operator
  --> query:1:21
  |
1 | from t | where !b&&c||!d
  |                     ^^ this is C's "or"
  |
  = help: use `or` to accept either condition

error[DS0111]: `!` is not an operator
  --> query:1:23
  |
1 | from t | where !b&&c||!d
  |                       ^ this is C's "not"
  |
  = help: negate it with `not(...)`: `not(d)`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    from t | where not(b) and c or not(d)
which parses without errors
